The point probably sits in or near the Makarov Basin, between the Lomonosov Ridge and the Alpha Ridge. The basin floor is roughly 3,900–4,000 m deep. The point may lie partway up the Lomonosov Ridge slope, so I estimate somewhat shallower than the basin floor.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3600}

12262.text:
This point is in the open South Pacific, so it is water. I estimated the depth from approximate recall of seafloor geography, not from a bathymetry dataset.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge crest probably lies east of this point at this latitude. That would put the site on the Pacific plate flank, on seafloor perhaps 20–30 million years old. Typical depths for crust of that age are around 4,000 m, so I estimate about −4,000 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4000}

3174.text:
This point is most likely open water: it appears to fall within the Kuybyshev Reservoir on the Volga, south of Kazan near where the Kama joins. That is a judgment from memory, not checked against a map. If the reservoir is narrower than I think here, the point would be land at roughly 60–120 m.

The reservoir surface sits at about 53 m. I've assumed a typical depth there of roughly 10–20 m, which puts the lakebed around 38 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": 38}

543.text:
This point is in the Arctic Ocean. I estimated the depth from approximate knowledge of the seafloor rather than from a bathymetry lookup, so it is uncertain.

The location seems to fall near the Mendeleev Ridge, where crests lie around 1,500–2,000 m deep, close to the Makarov Basin edge at about 3,900 m deep. I chose a value between those two.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -2500}

1335.text:
The point lies on the Greenland ice sheet. At 75°N the east coast is around 18–20°W, so 29°W is roughly 250 km inland on grounded ice.

For elevation I interpolated from two reference points. The NGRIP ice core site (75.1°N, 42.3°W) sits near the ice divide at about 2917 m. The surface drops toward the east coast, and 29°W is about 350 km east of NGRIP. That suggests an ice-surface elevation of roughly 2300 m. This is an estimate from reasoning, not a looked-up value.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 2300}

6437.text:
I estimate this point is on land in central Myanmar, at about 120 m elevation.

At 19°N the Rakhine coast lies near 94.0–94.5°E (Sandoway is about 18.46°N, 94.37°E). So 95.0°E falls east of the Arakan (Rakhine) Yoma range, in the western Irrawaddy basin. It sits just west of the Irrawaddy River, which runs near 95.2°E at this latitude, close to Thayet. That places the point in the lowland foothills. This is a rough estimate from my knowledge of the region, not a lookup in an elevation dataset: I'd put the likely range at 100–200 m and chose 120 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 120}

11392.text:
The point is about 160 km off the Chilean coast, so it is open ocean. Concepción, at roughly the same latitude, sits near 73.05°W, and 75°W lies well west of that.

From memory, the Peru-Chile Trench axis at 37°S runs near 74°W. That puts this point just seaward of the trench, on the Nazca Plate's outer rise. Typical depths there are about 4,000–4,500 m, so I estimate around −4,300 m. This is an approximation, not a value read from a bathymetric dataset.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}

851.text:
This point is most likely open water in the northern Kara Sea. Nearby land doesn't reach it. Franz Josef Land lies at about 45–65°E. Ushakov Island is at about 80.8°N, 79.3°E. Schmidt Island is at about 81.1°N, 90.9°E. Severnaya Zemlya lies further east, at about 95–107°E. So 81°N, 83°E sits in the gap between these.

The seabed depth is a rough estimate. The point is near the outer Kara Sea shelf, close to the shelf break at about 81.5°N. Depths there are probably around 200–400 m, so I've taken a midpoint of about −300 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -300}

4150.text:
The point at 43°N, 159°W lies in the open North Pacific, north of Hawaii and south of the Aleutians, so the surface type is Water.

The deep ocean floor in this region is typically about 5,400 to 5,800 m deep. The Emperor Seamounts lie farther west, near 170°E, so they do not affect this location. I estimate the seabed at about −5,600 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5600}

6479.text:
I'm estimating from general knowledge of Pacific seafloor depths, not a lookup, so the elevation is approximate.

This point is open ocean in the central Pacific, west of Hawaii and near the eastern end of the Mid-Pacific Mountains. Typical abyssal depths here are about 5,000–5,500 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5200}

15829.text:
This point appears to be on the East Antarctic polar plateau, just inland of the Transantarctic Mountains. That puts it on grounded ice, so the surface type is Solid.

I recall that the Beardmore Glacier is near 83.5°S, 170°E and the Dominion Range near 85.3°S, 166°E. At 85°S, 159°E, the location falls west of those features on the high plateau. Ice-surface elevations there are typically about 2,500–2,800 m, and I've taken a value near the middle of that range. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a lookup in an elevation dataset.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 2600}

11908.text:
This point is open ocean in the South Pacific, so the surface type is Water. The depth is an estimate from general seafloor structure, not from a looked-up value.

I place the site on the Pacific plate, west of the spreading ridge. I judge it a few hundred to roughly a thousand kilometres from the ridge axis. Seafloor at that distance from a ridge typically lies about 3,800–4,200 m deep, so I take a middle value of about −3,900 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3900}

5562.text:
This point is in the western Pacific, east of the Bonin (Ogasawara) Islands. I'm placing it from memory rather than from a bathymetry dataset, so the depth is approximate.

The Izu-Bonin Trench axis at this latitude lies roughly near 143.5°E. That puts 145°E just east of the trench, on the Pacific plate's outer rise. Abyssal depths there are typically around 5,700–6,000 m, so I estimate about −5,800 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5800}

8176.text:
This point is in the open equatorial Atlantic, so it is water. My best guess for the seabed is about 4,200 m below sea level, but this is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

The location sits near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. It lies between the St. Paul Fracture Zone (around 1°N) and the Romanche Fracture Zone (around 0°, 18°W). It is likely on the ridge flank, where typical depths are around 4,000 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4200}

14391.text:
This point is probably open ocean, about 150 km off the Oates/Pennell coast of East Antarctica. The coastline near 163°E sits at roughly 70.5°S, judging from Rennick Bay and Lillie Glacier.

At 69°S the site likely lies on the continental slope beyond the shelf break. The depth is a rough estimate rather than a looked-up value; the slope there could plausibly range from about −1,000 to −2,500 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -1600}

1053.text:
This point is most likely open ocean on the Laptev Sea continental slope, so I've estimated a seabed depth of about 2,200 m below sea level. This is an estimate from my general knowledge of the region's geography, not a lookup in a bathymetric dataset.

At 79°N, 127°E the location sits in the northern Laptev Sea. It is well north of the New Siberian Islands (around 75°N) and east of Severnaya Zemlya (around 95–105°E), so it is not on land. The Laptev shelf edge lies roughly at 77–78°N in this longitude range, so 79°N should be past the shelf break, on the slope descending toward the Amundsen Basin. The end of the Gakkel Ridge also reaches the shelf near here. I placed the depth around 2,000–2,500 m, but it could be noticeably shallower or deeper.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -2200}

2932.text:
I estimate this is land at about 200 m, but the point is close to Lac Minto, so it could be water instead.

This point is in the interior of northern Quebec, roughly 100 km inland from the Hudson Bay coast, which lies near 76.7°W at this latitude. Lac Minto sits around 57.0–57.5°N and 74.5–75.8°W, so it may cover this exact spot. I've assumed land just south of the lake. If it is lake, the answer would be water with a lakebed somewhat below the lake surface of about 168 m. The 200 m figure is an estimate for the surrounding terrain, not a looked-up value.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 200}

712.text:
This point is in the Arctic Ocean, so the answer is a seabed depth. I placed it from my recollection of Arctic seafloor geography, not a bathymetric dataset, so the depth is approximate.

The Lomonosov Ridge runs from near Greenland, through the North Pole, toward the New Siberian Islands at roughly 140°E. At 165°E and 83°N, the point should lie on the Amerasian side of the ridge, in the Makarov Basin. That basin is generally about 3,800–4,000 m deep, so I estimate the seabed at about -3,800 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3800}

208.text:
This point is in the Arctic Ocean, so the answer is Water. The depth is an estimate from memory of Arctic seafloor geography, not a lookup in a bathymetry dataset.

87°N, 123°W lies about 330 km from the North Pole. It is on the North American side of the Lomonosov Ridge, the undersea ridge that runs across the pole from Greenland toward Siberia. That side is generally the deep Makarov Basin, which is typically about 3,900–4,000 m deep near the pole. I put the seabed at roughly 3,950 m below sea level. The real value could differ by a few hundred meters.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3950}

4100.text:
This point is in Mongolia's Gobi-Altai region, on land. I estimated the elevation from nearby landmarks rather than a precise lookup, so it is approximate.

It lies roughly between the Ikh Bogd massif (about 3,957 m, to the west) and Baga Bogd (to the east). Orog Nuur lake, at about 1,217 m, is just to the northwest. That suggests the point sits on foothill slopes at about 1,500–1,800 m. I picked 1,600 m within that range.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 1600}

2736.text:
Northern Saskatchewan, just south of the south shore of Lake Athabasca, is my best estimate for this point. Fond du Lac sits on the north shore at about 59.32°N, 107.17°W, and the lake narrows around 107°W. So the south shore there is probably near 59.1–59.2°N, which would put 59.0°N on land.

The lake surface is about 213 m. The terrain just south of it rises somewhat, so I estimate about 260 m. This is approximate, not from a measured dataset, and the point could be close to the shoreline.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 260}

290.text:
87°N, 41°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean, so the surface type is Water. This is an estimate from my rough memory of Arctic seafloor geography, not a looked-up depth.

My understanding is that the Gakkel Ridge passes south of this point at this longitude. That places the location on the floor of the Amundsen Basin, which is typically about 4,000–4,500 m deep. I've taken a value near the middle of that range.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}
